# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): the activities and sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The deck was checked by machine (scripts checking the counts, the ids, that every back is Arabic letters and vowel marks only, NFC and the uniqueness of every front in every language; the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Ranking. Every word of wordfreq 3.1.1's Arabic list (the 100 000 most frequent, from Wikipedia, film and TV subtitles, news, the web and Twitter) was matched, without its vowel marks and, where needed, without its clitics (و ف ب ل ك ال, the future سـ, the object and possessive suffixes), against every form of every Arabic noun, verb, adjective and function word in English Wiktionary, inflection tables included. Its frequency was shared among the entries it matched, weighted towards dictionary and principal forms and against entries whose senses are all archaic or rare, and the entries were ranked by the frequency they collected."@en ,
                 "Selection. An AI agent chose the 1000 nouns a learner of Modern Standard Arabic needs, the corpus rank its main evidence, dropping the method's artifacts (a rare word credited with a particle's frequency, such as مَنّ 'aphid' with the preposition من), archaic, Classical-only, rare, dialect-only and vulgar words, proper names, and plurals and collectives filed as entries of their own (أَيَّام), adverbs filed as nouns (الْآنَ), nisba words that belong with the adjectives, and much of the verbal-noun abstraction and political jargon the ranking favors, to make room for everyday words (food, family, the body, the home, animals, weather, days and seasons, transport, jobs, school). The order is the ranking's, moved by hand only where a homograph inflated or buried a word."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The back is English Wiktionary's headword, the singular, without the article, fully voweled as Wiktionary gives it, at a pause (no case ending on nouns and adjectives). The note gives the headword's transliteration, Wiktionary's own converted to ALA-LC (ā ī ū, th kh dh sh gh, ʻ for ayn, ʼ for hamza, no initial hamza, ta marbuta -ah, a final -iyy as -ī), and forms chosen from Wiktionary's entry: the plural (the one or two in common use; none for a noun without a usual plural), and the gender where the ending does not show it (شَمْس is feminine, خَلِيفَة masculine), each in Arabic and transliterated; the note's labels are in the reader's language, the forms the same in all. The front gives one to three meanings in each of six languages, the most common sense first, written by AI agents from the sense chosen and Wiktionary's glosses, verbs as infinitives (To write, Att skriva, schreiben, Escribir, Écrire, 쓰다). Bracketed qualifiers tell apart words that would otherwise share a front, near-synonyms mostly. Every front is unique in each language in the deck."@en ,
                 "Card ids are the ALA-LC transliteration in ASCII letters, without diacritics, ʻ, ʼ or hyphens (كِتَاب kitab, كَتَبَ kataba, كَبِير kabir, مَدِينَة madinah); a clash gets -2, then -3, in rank order. They are addresses, not shown as text."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck is CC BY-SA 4.0. Its ranking uses wordfreq's data, CC BY-SA 4.0. Its Arabic, forms and transliterations, and the English glosses its meanings were written from, come from English Wiktionary, CC BY-SA 4.0, through Wiktextract's extract on kaikki.org. The share-alike sources set the deck's licence; both are credited here."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Selection (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The 1000 nouns, chosen by an AI agent from the ranking and checked against every candidate's Wiktionary entry; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: About 30 artifacts of the ranking dropped (the three entries for مَنّ, إِلًى, أَنَّة, بَيْن, تَمّ and the like), with plurals filed as nouns, adverbs, nisba words and most nationality words; about 380 first-pass picks, mostly verbal-noun abstractions and political jargon, replaced by everyday words from further down; six inflated words moved down (فَم from first to about 600). Where Wiktionary's first sense is dated, the modern one is taught (هَاتِف telephone). Wiktionary's Arabic has no entries the method could reach for rice, vegetables, soup, Monday, Wednesday and a few more, so the deck lacks them."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Fronts and forms in six languages (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every card's six fronts and the forms its note shows: written by one AI agent per 100 cards, then checked by a second that back-translated every front into Arabic against Wiktionary's glosses and checked every form and the Arabic; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 59 corrections over the 1000 cards, 44 of them to fronts (wrong or rare senses, unnatural words, style), the others to the forms shown; 80 doubts about the Arabic passed on to the next round."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Arabic forms (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every doubt the writers and reviewers raised about a card's Arabic (its voweling, transliteration or the forms its note shows): decided by an AI agent against English Wiktionary, and every change checked letter by letter and mark by mark by a second agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Doubts about 79 cards: 73 changed (mostly a plural Wiktionary's headword line lacks, added from its entry, and a few vowel marks and transliterations), 6 kept; the verifier rejected one change, a transliteration (tiknūlūjyā), which was corrected. A final full-deck review of every back and note by another AI agent found 13 more errors, among them the gender of عَرُوس and five plurals missing a vowel mark, all fixed, and the ALA-LC spelling of a medial ِيّ (īy, not iyy) made uniform by script."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Distinct fronts and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole deck: fronts shared by two cards in any language, found by script and told apart by an AI agent; then the scripts' checks, the library checker (npm run library) with the app's shapes, DCAT-AP and SKOS, and a second SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck)."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 64 fronts were shared by two cards in some language; 27 cards got a new front in one or more languages, a qualifier mostly (Mother (formal) for وَالِدَة beside أُمّ). A final review of the whole deck, one AI agent per language, then changed 21 fronts (Swedish 4, Korean 3, German 6, Spanish 5, French 3). The release conforms: the scripts found every front unique in each language, every back and form fully voweled Arabic in NFC and every id unique; the library checker found the library valid with this deck in it, and pySHACL found all 228 documents it checks conform."@en .

<https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq>
    dcterms:title "wordfreq 3.1.1 (Arabic word frequencies from Wikipedia, film and TV subtitles, news, the web and Twitter)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Robyn Speer" ;
